1. `round(i/2.0)` : round off as per actuall mathematics rules.
    and for negative numbers like round(-2.5) round down to -3.0.
    
    
2. In C++ make sure that when you want any operational answer in 
   floating point number and one operand is integer then use other
   operand floating point number strictly because immediately during
   the operation number's decimal part will be chopped off . 
	1. 'round(1/2)' : expecting 1 but it will give round(0) that is 0.
	so for getting 1 use round(1/2.0).
	
	
3. `modulo %` : use for taking remainder generally but you can use this
    only with integers .
    
    
4. `Checking a expression or a number is odd or not` : use '&' this is 
    binary and so this will comapre our number of expression on the basis 
    of its binary form . for even check number or expression with '1' and 
    for odd with '1'. As ((expression & 1) ==  1 ) if this evaluates to true
    then expression is odd , ((expression & 1) ==  0 ) if this evaluates to 
    true then expression is even.  These paranthesis are must with expression
    & 1 because == opeators is more powerful so this give buggy output.This
    expression will compare last digit only and for even numbers in binary 
    form their last digit end with 0 and for odd numbers they end with 1.
